  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Isolated showers and thunderstorms possible \nthrough the day and into tonight, but the risk of hazardous \nweather is low.\n\nRising heights and jet dynamics aloft early in the day should\nwork to suppress convection until the afternoon or evening, \nwhen conditions will become more favorable for some isolated \nshowers and storms to develop. Instability will be low (less\nthan 1,000 J/kg) and lapse rates will be poor, but PWats are\nfairly high (over 1.5\"). So not expecting any significant or\nhazardous weather out of any convection today into tonight, \nhowever cannot totally rule out some brief heavy downpours or \nlocally heavy rainfall totaling around 1-2\". The greater \ncoverage of convection into the evening should be focused north \nof Philadelphia, where PoPs are greatest. Any convection \npersisting into the overnight period should wane toward dawn.\n\nHighs today will be similar to Sunday, ranging from the upper\n70s to mid 80s in most areas with similar humidity levels. Skies\nwill be mostly cloudy, but a lucky few could see some more\npersistent sun. Humid again tonight with lows in the mid 60s to\nlow 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Heat and humidity will build into the middle of\nthe week, along with daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms. There is an increasing threat of severe \nthunderstorms and potentially flash flooding during the middle \nof the week, namely Tuesday through Thursday.\n\nBroad upper level ridging returns to the eastern US early this\nweek, bringing in surface high pressure across the eastern US. \nAfternoon highs will creep back into the mid 80s to low 90s for \nmost areas, about 5 degrees above average for early September. \nDewpoints will also remain in the upper 60s to low 70s. The \nhottest day(s) looks to be Tuesday and/or Thursday, when high \ntemperatures could get into the mid 90s across Delmarva and into\nthe Philly metro. Resulting heat indices will be well into the \n90s to around 100 degrees. We will continue to keep an eye on \nthe potential for any Heat Advisories.\n\nOne other aspect to watch with this ridging pattern is the \npotential for thunderstorms to rotate around the periphery of \nthe ridge and the position of a frontal boundary during the \nmiddle of the week. Guidance maintains a consensus that the \npattern will be supportive of multiple rounds of thunderstorms, \nespecially during the Tuesday through Thursday time frame.\n\nSPC maintains a Slight Risk (2 of 5) for severe thunderstorms \nTuesday for portions of the area, and a Slight Risk has been\nintroduced for Wednesday. It should be noted that there have\nbeen some indications in the guidance that Wednesday's severe\npotential will be much more conditional, as a frontal boundary\nlooks to be draped across our area with potential for easterly\nwinds and marine influence at the surface. This doesn't mean\nthere would be zero severe potential, but adds a further degree\nof uncertainty to Wednesday's setup. Thursday remains \nhighlighted with 15% probabilities for severe thunderstorms. The\nnorthwest flow pattern that will be in place will be supportive\nof all severe threats, including damaging winds, large hail, \nand tornadoes. The threat for flash flooding is typically lower \nwith these patterns, however given multiple days with the \npotential for storms and recent heavy rainfalls, at least some \nrisk for flash flooding will exist. \n\nLong story short, the Tuesday through Thursday period looks \nvery active with multiple rounds of thunderstorms across the \nregion, but details still remain unclear. Stay tuned for \nupdates on these threats over the coming days.\n\nBy Friday, current model consensus indicates the frontal \nboundary will be pushing into and south of our region with \nCanadian high pressure building across the Northeast. This \nshould favor an end to severe weather and flash flooding \npotential. Labor Day weekend is still shaping up to feature nice\nweather, with temperatures trending slightly below normal and\nlower humidity.",
